<!-- ============================================================
     CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT Detection Rules
     Threat: CylindricalCanine (GoldenEyeDog subgroup, APT-Q-27 /
             Dragon Breath / Miuuti Group); China-nexus cybercrime
             and espionage collective. Golden Gh0st RAT uses a
             dual-port WebSocket C2 architecture:
               TCP 5188: unencrypted command-and-control channel
               TCP 5198: encrypted data exfiltration channel

             DigiCert certificate breach (April 2026): 60 stolen
             code-signing certificates linked to 27+ signed malware
             specimens (Golden Gh0st Loader and Golden Gh0st RAT),
             defeating signature-based AV detection at deployment.

     Known C2 Domains:
       uu.goldeyeuu[.]io   (primary, unencrypted commands, TCP 5188)
       api.keensie[.]com   (encrypted exfiltration, TCP 5198)

     Known IOC SHA256 Hash:
       81e276aaa3eb9b3f595663c316b3c6414cc3dde5e6cc3a82856b7276acabb7de

     MITRE ATT&amp;CK Techniques:
       T1071.001 - Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols (TA0011)
       T1573     - Encrypted Channel (TCP 5198 exfil) (TA0011)
       T1041     - Exfiltration over C2 Channel (TA0010)
       T1553.002 - Code Signing (stolen DigiCert certificates) (TA0005)

     Rule IDs: 104089-104095
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Issue: ESKA-595
     Date: 2026-07-21

     Log source prerequisites:
       Rules 104089, 104090, 104091, 104092, 104093 (network):
         Firewall or network appliance logs in the Wazuh firewall
         group (SID 103000). FortiGate, pfSense, Suricata, and
         UniFi all qualify. Rule 104089 matches on destination
         hostname; rules 104091-104093 match on destination port
         in the raw firewall log. The port patterns dstport=5188
         and dstport=5198 cover FortiGate CEF key-value format;
         adjust the match string if your firewall uses a different
         key (dport=, destination_port=, DestPort=, or similar).
       Rule 104094 (process-level, Windows):
         Sysmon EventID 3 (NetworkConnect) forwarded to Wazuh.
         EventID 3 logging must be enabled in the Sysmon config
         and the Wazuh agent on the Windows host must forward the
         Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational event channel.
         Provides process attribution (which binary initiated the
         outbound connection) in addition to IP and port.
       Rule 104095 (FIM):
         Wazuh syscheck with SHA-256 checksums enabled
         (check_sha256sum="yes" in ossec.conf syscheck block).
         Fires when a file matching the known Golden Gh0st RAT
         binary hash appears as a new or modified file on any
         monitored host. Requires Wazuh 4.3+ for the
         syscheck.sha256_after decoded field.

     Severity tiers:
        0  - (none; no anchor rule needed for this ruleset)
       12  - Outbound TCP 5188 (low-confidence port-only match)
       13  - Outbound TCP 5198 (encrypted exfil channel)
       14  - Connection to known C2 domain (single event, high conf)
       14  - Sysmon EventID 3: non-browser connecting to 5188/5198
       15  - C2 domain beaconing: 2+ in 120 s, active implant
       15  - TCP 5188 beaconing: 3+ in 120 s, active RAT confirmed
       15  - Known Golden Gh0st RAT SHA256 hash detected via FIM

     References:
       Expel (2026-07-20): https://expel.com/blog/introducing-cylindricalcanine/
       The Hacker News (2026-07):
         https://thehackernews.com/2026/07/goldeneyedog-subgroup-linked-to.html
       MITRE ATT&amp;CK Dragon Breath G1071:
         https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1071/
       MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1071.001:
         https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071/001/
       MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1553.002:
         https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1553/002/
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="threat_intel,command_and_control,windows,linux,macos,cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,dragon_breath,china_nexus,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104089: Known C2 domain match in network/firewall logs
       Network/firewall logs - Wazuh SID 103000
       MITRE: T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols)

       Golden Gh0st RAT connects to two confirmed C2 domains:
         uu.goldeyeuu[.]io  (TCP 5188, unencrypted command channel)
         api.keensie[.]com  (TCP 5198, encrypted exfil channel)

       Both domains are registered CylindricalCanine infrastructure.
       They appear in outbound DNS queries and network connections
       from infected endpoints. CylindricalCanine used 60 stolen
       DigiCert code-signing certificates to sign the RAT and its
       loader, meaning AV may have allowed execution at time of
       infection. A connection to either domain indicates a running
       Golden Gh0st RAT implant.

       False positives: essentially none. These domains exist only
       as CylindricalCanine C2 infrastructure; any sighting is a
       confirmed infection. Isolate the originating host and begin
       incident response immediately.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104089" level="14">
    <if_sid>103000</if_sid>
    <match>uu\.goldeyeuu\.io|api\.keensie\.com</match>
    <description>CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT: outbound connection to confirmed C2 domain from $(agent.name) -- C2 domains uu.goldeyeuu[.]io (TCP 5188, unencrypted command channel) and api.keensie[.]com (TCP 5198, encrypted exfil channel) are attributed to CylindricalCanine, a GoldenEyeDog (APT-Q-27/Dragon Breath) China-nexus subgroup; 27+ malware specimens signed with 60 stolen DigiCert certificates bypass AV at deployment; isolate originating host and initiate IR immediately (T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
      <id>T1553.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,ioc_c2,ioc_domain,china_nexus,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104090: Sustained C2 domain beaconing (active RAT)
       Frequency rule on 104089 - 2+ events in 120 s, same source
       MITRE: T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols)
              T1573 (Encrypted Channel)

       Golden Gh0st RAT maintains persistent WebSocket connections
       to its C2 on TCP 5188 and TCP 5198 and reconnects at regular
       intervals. Two or more connections from the same source host
       to either C2 domain within 120 seconds is unambiguous
       confirmation of a running Golden Gh0st RAT implant performing
       active C2 check-ins.

       Response: Isolate the host immediately. Capture volatile
       memory before powering off. Review all data accessible from
       the endpoint and treat it as potentially staged for exfiltration.
       Given China-nexus attribution, escalate to CISO and IR.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104090" level="15" frequency="2" timeframe="120">
    <if_matched_sid>104089</if_matched_sid>
    <same_source_ip />
    <description>CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT CRITICAL: active C2 beaconing confirmed from $(agent.name) -- 2+ connections to Golden Gh0st C2 infrastructure (uu.goldeyeuu[.]io or api.keensie[.]com) within 120 seconds from the same host; Golden Gh0st RAT implant is running and performing regular C2 check-ins; China-nexus CylindricalCanine (GoldenEyeDog/APT-Q-27/Dragon Breath) espionage campaign; CRITICAL: isolate host NOW, capture memory, escalate to IR (T1071.001, T1573)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
      <id>T1573</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,ioc_c2,beaconing,active_rat,china_nexus,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104091: Outbound connection to TCP 5188 (command channel)
       Network/firewall logs - Wazuh SID 103000
       MITRE: T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols)

       TCP 5188 is the unencrypted WebSocket command channel used by
       Golden Gh0st RAT. The attacker sends commands over this port:
       keylogging tasks, file enumeration, process execution, and
       screenshot capture. TCP 5188 is IANA unassigned and not used
       by any recognized enterprise application. Outbound connections
       to external hosts on TCP 5188 from corporate endpoints are
       highly anomalous.

       This rule is the base anchor for the TCP 5188 family. Frequency
       rule 104093 fires when sustained beaconing on TCP 5188 is
       detected (3+ connections in 120 seconds from the same host).

       Port matching covers FortiGate CEF format (dstport=), common
       alternative log key names, and colon-prefixed port notation.
       Adjust if your firewall log uses a different field name.
       Blocked outbound connections still indicate an infection attempt.

       False positives: legitimate software using TCP 5188 (rare;
       IANA unassigned). Investigate any hit: check source process,
       destination, and correlate with domain rules 104089-104090.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104091" level="12">
    <if_sid>103000</if_sid>
    <match>dstport=5188|dport=5188|dst_port=5188|destination_port=5188|DestinationPort=5188|:5188 |:5188$</match>
    <description>CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT: outbound TCP 5188 connection from $(agent.name) -- TCP 5188 is the unencrypted WebSocket command channel for Golden Gh0st RAT attributed to CylindricalCanine (GoldenEyeDog/APT-Q-27/Dragon Breath); the attacker sends keylogging, file enumeration, and remote execution commands over this channel; TCP 5188 is IANA unassigned with no known legitimate enterprise use; triage source host and correlate with C2 domain rules 104089-104090 (T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,tcp_5188,command_channel,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104092: Outbound connection to TCP 5198 (encrypted exfil)
       Network/firewall logs - Wazuh SID 103000
       MITRE: T1573 (Encrypted Channel), T1041 (Exfiltration over C2)

       TCP 5198 is the encrypted WebSocket data exfiltration channel
       used by Golden Gh0st RAT. This channel carries encrypted
       payloads: harvested credentials, keylog data, screenshots,
       and file contents exfiltrated to CylindricalCanine C2
       infrastructure. The encryption means DPI will not reveal
       the content; detection must rely on the port number and
       destination host.

       TCP 5198 is IANA unassigned and used by no known legitimate
       enterprise application. Any outbound connection to an external
       host on TCP 5198 is a strong indicator of Golden Gh0st RAT
       exfiltration activity. The elevated severity (level 13 vs.
       level 12 for TCP 5188) reflects that data already flowing on
       the exfil channel means collection has occurred and exfiltration
       is in progress, increasing urgency for host isolation.

       Correlate with TCP 5188 activity (rule 104091) and known C2
       domain hits (rule 104089) on the same host to confirm the
       Golden Gh0st dual-port C2 pattern.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104092" level="13">
    <if_sid>103000</if_sid>
    <match>dstport=5198|dport=5198|dst_port=5198|destination_port=5198|DestinationPort=5198|:5198 |:5198$</match>
    <description>CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT: outbound TCP 5198 encrypted exfil connection from $(agent.name) -- TCP 5198 is the encrypted WebSocket exfiltration channel for Golden Gh0st RAT; CylindricalCanine (GoldenEyeDog/APT-Q-27/Dragon Breath) uses this channel to exfiltrate keylog data, credentials, screenshots, and files to C2 infrastructure; TCP 5198 is IANA unassigned with no legitimate enterprise use; isolate host and check for correlated TCP 5188 activity (rule 104091) and C2 domain hits (rule 104089) (T1573, T1041)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1573</id>
      <id>T1041</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,tcp_5198,exfil_channel,encrypted_channel,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104093: Sustained TCP 5188 beaconing (active RAT confirmed)
       Frequency rule on 104091 - 3+ events in 120 s, same source
       MITRE: T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols)

       Golden Gh0st RAT maintains a persistent WebSocket connection
       on TCP 5188 and reconnects when the session is dropped.
       Three or more outbound connections from the same source host
       to external TCP 5188 within 120 seconds is strong evidence of
       an active Golden Gh0st RAT implant maintaining its unencrypted
       command channel and awaiting attacker instructions.

       Sustained beaconing means the host is under active adversary
       control. Given China-nexus attribution (CylindricalCanine /
       Dragon Breath / APT-Q-27), the target may be subject to
       ongoing espionage operations. All data accessible from the
       host should be treated as potentially staged for exfiltration
       via the TCP 5198 channel.

       Response: Isolate host immediately. Capture volatile memory.
       Preserve process list, open connections, and endpoint logs
       before powering off. Notify CISO and begin IR.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104093" level="15" frequency="3" timeframe="120">
    <if_matched_sid>104091</if_matched_sid>
    <same_source_ip />
    <description>CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT CRITICAL: active C2 beaconing confirmed via TCP 5188 from $(agent.name) -- 3+ outbound connections to TCP 5188 (Golden Gh0st unencrypted command channel) within 120 seconds; active Golden Gh0st RAT implant is maintaining its C2 channel awaiting attacker commands; China-nexus CylindricalCanine (GoldenEyeDog/APT-Q-27/Dragon Breath) espionage; CRITICAL: isolate host NOW, capture memory, treat all accessible data as potentially exfiltrated via TCP 5198, escalate to IR (T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,tcp_5188,beaconing,active_rat,china_nexus,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104094: Sysmon EventID 3 connection to TCP 5188 or 5198
       Sysmon EventID 3 (NetworkConnect) - Wazuh SID 61644
       MITRE: T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols)
              T1573 (Encrypted Channel)

       Sysmon EventID 3 (NetworkConnect) records process-level
       attribution for outbound TCP connections on Windows. This
       rule fires when a non-browser process initiates a connection
       to TCP 5188 (Golden Gh0st command channel) or TCP 5198
       (Golden Gh0st encrypted exfil channel) on any destination.

       The browser negate eliminates the rare edge case of a browser
       connecting to these ports via a WebSocket-based web app.
       No known legitimate browser extension uses TCP 5188 or 5198
       externally. The connecting process name and image path
       captured by Sysmon provide a direct path to identifying the
       Golden Gh0st RAT binary on disk for hash verification and
       quarantine.

       Important: Golden Gh0st RAT binaries are signed with stolen
       DigiCert certificates. The file will appear digitally signed
       by a plausible-looking vendor. Do not dismiss this alert
       because the binary is signed; verify the signing certificate
       serial number against the 60 stolen DigiCert certs from the
       April 2026 breach. Escalate if the issuer is DigiCert but
       the subject organization is unfamiliar.

       Requires Sysmon EventID 3 logging enabled in the Sysmon
       configuration and the Wazuh agent forwarding the
       Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational channel.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104094" level="14">
    <if_sid>61644</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^3$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.destinationPort" type="pcre2">^(?:5188|5198)$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:chrome|firefox|msedge|safari|opera|brave|iexplore|chromium|Electron)\.exe$</field>
    <description>CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT: non-browser process $(win.eventdata.image) initiated TCP $(win.eventdata.destinationPort) connection to $(win.eventdata.destinationIp) on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) -- Sysmon EventID 3 provides process attribution for Golden Gh0st RAT WebSocket C2 connection; TCP 5188 is the unencrypted command channel and TCP 5198 is the encrypted exfiltration channel; Golden Gh0st RAT binaries are signed with stolen DigiCert certificates (April 2026 breach) so the signing certificate will appear valid; verify signing cert subject and serial number against the stolen DigiCert cert list (T1071.001, T1573)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
      <id>T1573</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,windows,sysmon,tcp_5188,tcp_5198,process_attribution,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104095: FIM SHA256 match for known Golden Gh0st RAT binary
       Wazuh FIM syscheck - SID 550,553,554,593
       MITRE: T1553.002 (Code Signing - defense evasion via stolen cert)

       This rule detects the confirmed SHA256 hash of a known Golden
       Gh0st RAT binary specimen:
         81e276aaa3eb9b3f595663c316b3c6414cc3dde5e6cc3a82856b7276acabb7de

       CylindricalCanine signed Golden Gh0st RAT and its loaders
       using 60 code-signing certificates stolen from DigiCert
       in April 2026. Standard AV engines that trust Authenticode
       signatures may have allowed the binary to execute without
       alerting. The SHA256 IOC provides a hash-based detection path
       independent of the signing certificate: if Wazuh FIM syscheck
       is configured with SHA-256 checksums, it will alert when this
       binary is written to or detected on any monitored path.

       The syscheck.sha256_after field contains the SHA-256 of the
       file at the time of the FIM event. A match on this hash is a
       confirmed Golden Gh0st RAT binary. This is a CRITICAL alert
       regardless of file location, signing status, or whether the
       binary has been executed yet. Quarantine the file and isolate
       the host before checking whether the RAT is actively running.

       Requires Wazuh 4.3+ with check_sha256sum="yes" in the ossec.conf
       syscheck block. Recommended monitored paths:
         Windows: C:\Windows\Temp, C:\Users\*\AppData, C:\ProgramData,
                  C:\Windows\System32 (with appropriate scope limits)
         Linux:   /tmp, /var/tmp, /opt, /usr/local/bin
         macOS:   /tmp, /Users/*/Library, /usr/local

       False positives: none. This is a confirmed malicious file hash.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104095" level="15">
    <if_sid>550,553,554,593</if_sid>
    <field name="syscheck.sha256_after">81e276aaa3eb9b3f595663c316b3c6414cc3dde5e6cc3a82856b7276acabb7de</field>
    <description>CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT CRITICAL: confirmed malicious binary hash detected via FIM on $(agent.name) at $(syscheck.path) -- SHA256 hash 81e276aaa3eb9b3f595663c316b3c6414cc3dde5e6cc3a82856b7276acabb7de is a known Golden Gh0st RAT specimen signed with a DigiCert certificate stolen in the April 2026 CylindricalCanine breach; the file appears digitally signed and AV may have permitted its execution; do not dismiss because the file appears signed; CRITICAL: quarantine file immediately, isolate host, and initiate IR (T1553.002, T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1553.002</id>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,ioc_hash,fim_hash_match,code_signing,critical,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
